# Skip the install while a rebase is replaying (merge backend; see the scope
# note below).
#
# Git checks out the base commit to start a rebase, which fires this hook. The
# install that follows rewrites bun.lock whenever the base commit's lockfile
# differs from what the installed Bun would write, leaving the tree dirty
# before the first commit is replayed, and the rebase stops with "cannot
# rebase: You have unstaged changes". Retrying re-runs the hook and re-dirties
# the tree, so the rebase cannot make progress without disabling hooks
# wholesale.
#
# This skip leaves a real window, so it does not stand alone. Measured on git
# 2.39.5: a rebase fires post-checkout exactly once — the base checkout skipped
# here — and fires nothing on completion. Skipping alone would mean a rebase
# that pulls in package.json or bun.lock changes resolves dependencies zero
# times. post-rewrite closes it: that hook does fire once the replay finishes,
# and installs there.
#
# `--git-path` rather than a literal .git/ path: it resolves correctly inside a
# worktree, where this state lives under .git/worktrees/<name>/.
#
# Only the merge backend — git's default since 2.26 — is guarded, and only a
# rebase at that. Two exemptions worth naming rather than leaving to be
# rediscovered:
#
#   `git rebase --apply` tears its state directory down BEFORE firing this
#   hook, so no state-file check can see it. That backend still dirties the
#   tree mid-replay and installs twice. It is opt-in (`rebase.backend` is
#   unset here and nothing in the repo invokes it), so it is left uncovered
#   rather than papered over with a check that cannot work.
#
#   merge, cherry-pick and revert never fire this hook at all, so guarding
#   their state files would be dead code implying a breadth of coverage this
#   hook does not have.
#
# `rebase --abort` is the one gap with no install: this hook is skipped on the
# way in and post-rewrite never fires, since nothing was rewritten. Harmless in
# practice — an abort restores the original HEAD, which is the tree
# node_modules already matched.
if [ -e "$(git rev-parse --git-path rebase-merge)" ] ||
   [ -e "$(git rev-parse --git-path rebase-apply)" ]; then
  exit 0
fi

if [ "$3" = "1" ]; then
  bun install
fi
